\(2^{\circ}\)) Let \(\mathbf{v}\in H\) and let \(\mathbf{\tilde{v}}\) denote its extension by zero outside \(\Omega\). Then \(\operatorname{div}\mathbf{\tilde{v}}=0\) in \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\) and since the support of \(\mathbf{\tilde{v}}\) is compact we can apply Theorem 3.4, i.e.: there exists \(\psi\in H^{1}(\mathbb{R}^{3})^{3}\) such that

\[\mathbf{\tilde{v}}=\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi,\quad\operatorname{div} \psi=0\quad\text{in }\mathbb{R}^{3}.\]

The idea is to construct first a function \(\mathbf{w}\) with vanishing divergence and rotation and with the same tangential components as \(\psi\). Then the difference \(\psi-\mathbf{w}\) will satisfy the first boundary condition in (3.28). The next step will consist in showing that among all such functions \(\mathbf{w}\) there is one which has the same average normal component on \(\Gamma_{i}\) as \(\psi\); and therefore their difference will satisfy all the requirements.

To begin with, we observe that

\[\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\psi=\mathbf{0}\quad\text{in }\mathbb{R}^{3}- \bar{\Omega}.\]

Now, let \(\mathcal{C}\) be an open ball containing \(\bar{\Omega}\) and let \(\Omega_{i}\) denote the component of \(\mathcal{C}-\bar{\Omega}\) bounded by \(\Gamma_{i}\) like in Figure 2. Since \(\Omega\) is simply-connected, each \(\Omega_{i}\) is also simply-connected (cf. for example Bernardi [8]) and therefore Theorem 2.9 implies that there exist \(q_{i}\in H^{1}(\Omega_{i})\) such that:

(3.30) \[\psi=\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}q_{i}\quad\text{in }\Omega_{i}\quad\text{for }0 \leqslant i\leqslant p.\]

In addition, \(q_{i}\in H^{2}(\Omega_{i})\) since \(\psi\in H^{1}(\mathcal{C})^{3}\). Next let \(\chi\in H^{1}(\Omega)\) be the solution of the non-homogeneous Dirichlet problem

(3.31) \[\begin{cases}\mathcal{A}\chi=0&\text{in }\Omega,\\ \chi|_{\Gamma_{i}}=q_{i}&\text{for }0\leqslant i\leqslant p\end{cases}\]

and set

\[\mathbf{q}=(q_{0},\dots,q_{p}).\]

Clearly, the mapping \(\mathbf{q}\to\chi\) belongs to \(\mathcal{L}(\prod_{i=0}^{p}H^{2}(\Omega_{i});H^{1}(\Omega))\) and the set \(\{\chi,q_{0},q_{1},\dots,q_{p}\}\) is an extension of \(\chi\) in \(H^{1}(\mathcal{C})\). Finally, we take

\[\mathbf{w}=\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}\chi;\]

we have:

\[\operatorname{div}\mathbf{w}=0,\quad\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\mathbf{w}= \mathbf{0}\quad\text{in }\Omega\]

and

\[\mathbf{w}\times\mathbf{n}=\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}\chi\times\mathbf{n}= \operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}q_{i}\times\mathbf{n}=\psi\times\mathbf{n}\quad \text{on }\Gamma_{i},\quad\ 0\leqslant i\leqslant p.\]

Hence, the function \(\psi-\mathbf{w}\) verifies (3.23) and the first condition in (3.28).

Of course, the function \(\mathbf{w}\) is not unique because (3.30) fixes each \(q_{i}\) up to an additive constant. Our purpose now is to prove that these \(p+1\) constants may be chosen so that

\[\langle(\psi-\mathbf{w})\mathbin{\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\! \!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!
